WF-01 — Identity & Security Gate

## Summary

- Purpose: First-stage triage for every inbound message from WF-00. WF-01 is now an identity/security gate ONLY — no messageType branching. It decides one of: silent-reject (foreign country), silent-drop (already blocked), route to re-engagement (opted_out), route to brand-new handler, or route to the state router (anyone with a record).
- Inputs: parsed WhatsApp message payload from WF-00 — structured block below.
- Outputs: Either a silent reject (country), a silent drop (blocked), a call to WF-26 (opted_out re-engagement), a call to WF-21 (brand-new, no record), or a call to WF-02 (has a `users` row OR a `pending_users` row). Every downstream call carries the canonical core envelope (design.md §2.1).
- **Envelope emission (data-contract-discipline §2.1):** every output branch that calls a downstream workflow (WF-02, WF-26, WF-21) emits the canonical core envelope `{ phoneNumber, messageType, messageContent, user:{id, phone_number, name, status, slack_channel_id, current_consultation_id} | null, pendingUser:{id, contact_name} | null }`, so leaves read these fields without re-SELECTing.
- State Transitions: none (read-only; does not mutate user state).
- Calls Sub-Workflows: WF-21 (brand-new), WF-26 (opted_out re-engagement), WF-02 (existing record).
- **Deltas vs the prior WF-01 (BMX-06 §5):**
  - REMOVED the Layer-2 non-text filter — WF-01 no longer drops non-text. Non-text now flows downstream and is handled per-stage by WF-02 / WF-21 / WF-23 (which escalate via U2).
  - REMOVED the `anomaly_keyword` (STOP/REBOOK-from-unrecorded-phone) routing — those now flow through to WF-21 (brand-new) and are preempted there.
  - REMOVED the `anomaly_interactive` routing — interactive taps from unrecorded phones flow to WF-21 too.
  - Block lookup is now the UNIFIED `status='blocked'` gate (the prior "blacklisted" coinage is gone; one terminal block state across admin BLOCK, abuse, and threshold auto-block).
  - Brand-new users route to WF-21 DIRECTLY (they no longer pass through WF-02).
- **Note — country filter:** the live allow-list is `{91 India, 61 Australia}` (Australia is the operator's region). The BMX-06 §5 shorthand "non-+91 → reject" is not intended to drop Australia; the live two-country allow-list is preserved.

## Inputs

**Parsed WhatsApp message payload from WF-00 (the WF-00 emit contract — Step 2 of WF-00.pseudo):**
- `phoneNumber` — string, required. Sender MSISDN, digits only. Used for the Step-4 status lookup.
- `phoneNumberFormatted` — string, required. `+`-prefixed E.164. Used for the Step-2 country filter.
- `messageType` — string, required. `text` | `interactive` | other. Forwarded into downstream envelopes.
- `messageContent` — string, required. Text body / interactive id / `[TYPE]` bracket. Forwarded into downstream envelopes.
- `messageContentUpper` — string, required. Uppercased-trimmed `messageContent`; consumed by `Classify & Build Envelope`.
- `messageId` — string, required. Meta message id; carried into downstream envelopes / logging.
- `timestamp` — string, required. Meta message timestamp; carried by `Classify & Build Envelope`.
- `interactiveLabel` — string, optional. Interactive display title; `null` otherwise.
- `contactName` — string, optional. WhatsApp profile name; `null` if absent.
- `rawMessage` — object, required. Raw Meta `message` object.
- `metadata` — object, required. `{ phoneNumberId, displayPhoneNumber }`; consumed by `Classify & Build Envelope`.

---

## Algorithm

Step 1: Start — triggered by WF-00 with the parsed message payload.
Step 2: Country Filter — check whether `phoneNumberFormatted` starts with an allowed country code (`91` India or `61` Australia).
  - If NOT allowed → go to Step 3 (silent reject).
  - If allowed → go to Step 4.
Step 3: Silent reject (country) — return `{ silentReject: true, reason: 'country_not_allowed' }`. End.
Step 4: Status Lookup — a single combined query resolving the phone's identity + security state: SELECT the `users` row (id, status, name, slack_channel_id, current_consultation_id, and the rest of the projected columns) and the `pending_users` row for `phoneNumber`. Derive `has_user`, `has_pending`, and `user_status`.
Step 5: Check: is the phone blocked (`has_user` AND `user_status == 'blocked'`)?
  - If YES → go to Step 6 (silent drop — already blocked; no log needed).
  - If NO → go to Step 7.
Step 6: Silent drop (blocked) — return `{ silentReject: true, reason: 'blocked' }`. No `silent_drop` log (the phone is already blocked). End.
Step 7: Check: is the phone opted out (`has_user` AND `user_status == 'opted_out'`)?
  - If YES → go to Step 8.
  - If NO → go to Step 9.
Step 8: Build the canonical §2.1 envelope from the loaded `users` row (`pendingUser: null` — opted_out users have no pending row by construction; carry `wasOptedOut: true` so WF-26's entry guard can assert the branch) and call WF-26 (Re-Engaged Opted-Out User Handler). WF-26 lifts the user to `consultation_closed` and re-routes the message through WF-02 so the safety net runs on the re-engagement message. End.
Step 9: Check: is the phone brand-new (NOT `has_user` AND NOT `has_pending`)?
  - If YES → go to Step 10.
  - If NO → go to Step 11.
Step 10: Build the canonical §2.1 envelope (`user: null`, `pendingUser: null`) and call WF-21 (Brand-New Owner) directly. Brand-new users no longer pass through WF-02. End.
Step 11: Build the canonical §2.1 envelope (`user` populated if `has_user`, else null; `pendingUser` populated if `has_pending`, else null) and call WF-02 (User State Router). End.
